---
// Lesson 8.2 — Boss Body, Health Bar & Phases
// BossManager: singleton health, UI bar, phase thresholds.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.2 — Boss Body, Health Bar &amp; Phases</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The spec exists (<a href="8.1.html">8.1</a>); today the boss
    gets a body, a number that says how alive it is, a BAR the player
    can watch fall, and the phase thresholds that make it change its
    mind. Health first, attacks later — a boss you cannot hurt cannot
    be tuned.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the body.</strong> Big sprite, big collider,
    on the "Enemy" physics layer (your <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a>
    layers pay off today: boss ignores boss, player passes
    decoration). If you have starter art, scale a mobile enemy up
    3×; a placeholder rectangle is also fine and honest.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the health, in the StatManager's voice.</strong>
    The starter keeps player stats in a singleton; your boss is a
    scene-local boss, so it owns its own numbers — new script,
    same style:</p>
    <pre><code>public class BossManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private int maxHealth = 20;
    [SerializeField] private int phaseTwoAt = 10;   // 50%
    [SerializeField] private int phaseThreeAt = 4;  // 20%
    public static BossManager boss;
    private int currentHealth;
    private int currentPhase = 1;

    private void Awake()
    {
        boss = this;
        currentHealth = maxHealth;
    }

    public void ChangeHealth(int amount)
    {
        currentHealth += amount;
        currentHealth = Mathf.Max(currentHealth, 0);

        if (currentHealth &lt;= 0)
        {
            Die();
        }
        else if (currentHealth &lt;= phaseThreeAt &amp;&amp; currentPhase == 2)
        {
            EnterPhase(3);
        }
        else if (currentHealth &lt;= phaseTwoAt &amp;&amp; currentPhase == 1)
        {
            EnterPhase(2);
        }
    }

    private void EnterPhase(int phase)
    {
        currentPhase = phase;
        Debug.Log("PHASE " + phase);
        // 8.4: each phase swaps attack cooldowns / movement
    }

    private void Die()
    {
        Debug.Log("BOSS DOWN");
        // 8.5: door opens, win path
        Destroy(gameObject);
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>Read the threshold logic out loud: phase changes only fire
    DOWNWARD (currentPhase == checks) — a heal in Phase 2 cannot
    re-trigger Phase 2's entrance. Why does the state guard matter
    here more than it did for the lever (<a href="5.2.html">5.2</a>)?</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the bar.</strong> UI the honest way: a Slider
    (GameObject → UI → Slider) anchored top-center. In
    BossManager:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private Slider healthBar;

private void Update()
{
    if (healthBar != null)
    {
        healthBar.maxValue = maxHealth;
        healthBar.value = currentHealth;
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>Why a bar instead of a number: the bar shows the
    <em>pace</em> of victory — the last sliver is drama a number
    cannot reach.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Phase bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Remove the currentPhase guards. Hit the boss from
                12 → 9 in one big hit (phase 2 fires) — then a
                heal... no, simpler: hit 9 → 8 → 7. How many times
                did EnterPhase(2) run, and what did the boss's
                entrance animation do?</li>
            <li>Set phaseTwoAt ABOVE maxHealth (say 30 of 20).
                What happens on the FIRST hit — and why is "phase
                thresholds are less than maxHealth" a spec
                invariant (<a href="8.1.html">8.1</a>
                arithmetic again)?</li>
            <li>Health goes negative from a huge hit
                (Mathf.Max removed). Phase 3 fires, then Die
                fires... but wait, which else-if ran first — and
                why did the boss SKIP phase 3's entrance entirely?
                Is that a bug or a feature (argue)?</li>
            <li>Two BossManager instances in the scene (you cloned
                the boss). Which one does <code>BossManager.boss</code>
                point at — and why did the starter's StatManager
                fight for singleton status in
                <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> too?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Phase feel</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Give EnterPhase a VISIBLE effect today, even before
                attacks: flash the sprite color, shake the camera
                (a transform jitter for 0.3s), play a sound. Why
                must the player KNOW the phase changed within one
                second?</li>
            <li>Tune your spec's arithmetic against the real bar:
                poke the boss with the debug console
                (BossManager.boss.ChangeHealth(-1)) — how many
                pokes to phase 2? Does the bar's pace match your
                8.1 prediction?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"8.2a: boss body + phases + bar"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build BossManager with guarded phase thresholds</li>
            <li>Wire a UI slider to boss health and explain bar-vs-number</li>
            <li>Explain why phase transitions fire once, downward only</li>
            <li>Test phase logic by poking health from the console</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight</a> — health, phases, UI</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Mathf, singletons</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="8.3.html">8.3 — Player Attack: Fight Back</a></p>
</div>`;
